  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging remains centered over the Quad State region\ntoday into early next week, while a large area of surface high\npressure has firm control over the northeast. Highs will\ncontinue to be in the mid to upper 70s with lows around the\nmid-40s. Some early morning fog is possible near rivers and \nlakes.\n\nEarly week, a disturbance moving from the Front Range to the\nNorthern Great Lakes will be well north of the Quad State\nregion. A second large surface high migrates from its current\nposition near Manitoba towards New England, reasserting high \npressure to our east midweek. Surface winds locally will be \nlight and generally out of the southeast, heavily influenced by \nthe eastern high. A midweek disturbance is likely to increase \nhigh cloud coverage locally but keep precipitation northwest of \nthe CWA. Temporarily slightly disrupting the ridge locally, \nridging reforms for late week, keeping dry conditions forecast.\n\nA trough digs into the western U.S. late week into next \nweekend. While the main trough axis is centered further west, a\nsmaller scale negatively tilted trough crossing the Central \nPlains Saturday is likely to advance close enough to the Quad\nState to finally bring a chance of rain to the region.\nInconveniently, this would be Halloween weekend so, once we move\ncloser, we will need to monitor this system for timing issues. \nTemperatures, forecast to be several degrees above normal \nthrough the seven day forecast period, are also favored to be \nabove normal in the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks. Completely dry\nconditions today through the workweek will give way to above \nnormal precipitation being favored in the 6-10 day outlook.",
